{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs as main carries it, at merge commit 0e0e71ad125985df31e07c15fcc0e5dec14cd9ea, which landed the documentation re-anchor approved by codex in 36325c53 and ratified in e14f67c9. (Records are named here by the trailing eight hex of their event id.)\n\nThe merge changed exactly twelve pages: docs/concepts/actors.md, docs/reference/event-identifiers.md, docs/reference/glossary.md, docs/reference/gs/actor-add.md, docs/reference/gs/actors.md, docs/reference/gs/provenance.md, docs/reference/gs/ratify.md, docs/reference/gs/review.md, docs/reference/gs/role-grant.md, docs/reference/gs/role-revoke.md, docs/reference/mcp/ratify.md and docs/reference/mcp/whoami.md. I took that set from git diff of the merge against its first parent rather than from a two-dot diff against the branch, which also showed cmd/gitseq-mcp and the citation baseline because the branch sat behind main.\n\nEleven of the changes are citation-only, repointing pages from older spike-path artifacts to their later successors at the same paths. The point is not tidiness: a page anchored to a record nothing will ever supersede cannot flare, so it goes quietly wrong when the behaviour moves underneath it. The twelfth is a prose correction in docs/reference/gs/actors.md — a retired principal stays on the roster with retired true and no roles, and local custody can still be true — which is what ActorViews passes through and what the fold regression asserts.\n\nGates run at this exact merge commit in the main checkout: go vet ./..., go build ./..., and the Go suite over ./internal/... and ./cmd/... with -count=1, with internal/docset run on its own rather than beside another suite. Results are in the report I gave the coordinator.\n\nSuccession at this path. This retires two live predecessors I authored: 66175dc3, the artifact for merge commit 8f0b86f3, and c02b180e, the candidate at head ee42fe05.\n\nIt also retires eleven narrower artifacts, one per changed page, at docs/concepts/actors.md, docs/reference/event-identifiers.md, docs/reference/glossary.md, docs/reference/gs/actor-add.md, docs/reference/gs/actors.md, docs/reference/gs/provenance.md, docs/reference/gs/ratify.md, docs/reference/gs/role-grant.md, docs/reference/gs/role-revoke.md, docs/reference/mcp/ratify.md and docs/reference/mcp/whoami.md. All eleven named commit a4f4a57a, which main contains, and each is retired bare, naming this path as the survivor. The wider path wins, and nothing will be published at those eleven strings again, so the area settles on one granularity rather than carrying both for ever. I checked first that no tracked file cites any of the thirteen: the twelve pages now cite ten ids, and none of them is a record this merge retires.\n\nTwo things stay live here, deliberately.\n\ne1cdfbe4 names commit 5caf6e85, which is not in main — it is the live head of request/merge-driven-succession in another session's worktree. It is a candidate for work still in flight, not a predecessor describing what main carries, and retiring it would stale a pending review and block that merge. Whoever lands that head should supersede it then.\n\n1721eb9b, 54018c23, d41408fd and eab525cc were recorded by codex. The fold admits a supersession only from the target's own author or from an actor holding ratifier, and claude is a bare participant, so codex or hugh must retire those; I will not sign as anyone else.\n\nIt rests on 3b7f205d at cmd/gs, live and not stale, and not on any record this merge retires or on the review of the merged head.","body":{"commit":"0e0e71ad125985df31e07c15fcc0e5dec14cd9ea","path":"docs"}}